        {
            "opId": "93492eeb-bf35-4a62-98f7-d85b0b79bfe5",
            "id": "dcr6sibet",
            "attribs": {},
            "uiAttribs": {
                "comment_title": "What is it ?",
                "comment_text": "Design interactive behavior as a graph, right \ninside your patch. Draw states, connect them with \nframe-timed transitions, and drop regions that \nbroadcast when they're active — then wire it all \ninto your visuals. Trigger transitions from \nanything in the patch, watch the active state, \nframe count, and transition countdown update live, \nand drive particles, scenes, or UI from a state \nmachine you can actually see. No spaghetti, no \nguesswork — just a clean, node-based brain for your \nproject.",
                "translate": {
                    "x": 545.7854119555915,
                    "y": -444.7224728758709
                },
                "color": "#07F78C"
            },
            "portsIn": [
                {
                    "name": "title",
                    "value": "What is it ?"
                },
                {
                    "name": "text",
                    "value": "Design interactive behavior as a graph, right \ninside your patch. Draw states, connect them with \nframe-timed transitions, and drop regions that \nbroadcast when they're active — then wire it all \ninto your visuals. Trigger transitions from \nanything in the patch, watch the active state, \nframe count, and transition countdown update live, \nand drive particles, scenes, or UI from a state \nmachine you can actually see. No spaghetti, no \nguesswork — just a clean, node-based brain for your \nproject."
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "opId": "93492eeb-bf35-4a62-98f7-d85b0b79bfe5",
            "id": "bb3za0t3r",
            "attribs": {},
            "uiAttribs": {
                "comment_title": "Why you should use a statemachine ?",
                "comment_text": "How a state machine works\nA finite state machine (FSM) models something that can be in exactly one of several defined states at a time, and moves between \nthem only along allowed transitions.\n\nThree pieces:\n\nStates — the distinct \"modes\" your thing can be in. Only one is active at any moment. (e.g. Idle, Playing, Paused.)\nTransitions — the permitted arrows from one state to another. A transition fires on an event/trigger \n(a click, a timer, a signal from elsewhere in your patch).\n\nThe active state — the current mode. Everything downstream reacts to which state is active.\nSo the whole logic reduces to: \"I'm in state X; when event Y happens, go to state Z.\" Nothing else can happen. \nA traffic light is the classic example — Green → Yellow → Red → Green, and it physically cannot jump Green → Red.\n\nIn your Automata op, that's exactly the graph: nodes are states, arrows are transitions \n(with an optional frame delay), and the active state drives your visuals/particles.\n\nWhy use one instead of ad-hoc logic\nThe alternative most people reach for is a pile of booleans — isPlaying, isPaused, hasStarted, menuOpen… \nThe problem is those flags can combine into impossible or contradictory situations (isPlaying && isPaused?), \nand the logic to keep them consistent balloons into tangled if/else.\n\nA state machine fixes that:\n\nOnly one state at a time — illegal combinations are structurally impossible.\nExplicit, legal transitions — you can't reach a state except through a defined path, so no surprise jumps.\nVisible & self-documenting — the graph is the logic. Anyone can look at it and understand the behavior \n(this is a big deal for your op, since it's literally drawn on screen).\nPredictable & debuggable — at any moment you know precisely where you are and what can happen next; \nbugs become \"wrong arrow\" instead of \"which of 12 flags is stale?\"\nEasy to extend — adding a mode is adding a node and a few arrows, not threading a new flag through every conditional.\nWhere it shines: anything with distinct modes and controlled flow — interactive installations \n(attract-loop → interaction → reset), game/UI screens (menu → play → pause), sequenced animations, or timed show control. \nExactly the kind of thing cables patches do, which is why having it as a visual node graph is so handy: \nyou get the rigor of an FSM without writing the bookkeeping by hand.",
                "translate": {
                    "x": 547.5105288206902,
                    "y": -143.21356961479893
                },
                "color": "#5dc0fd"
            },
            "portsIn": [
                {
                    "name": "title",
                    "value": "Why you should use a statemachine ?"
                },
                {
                    "name": "text",
                    "value": "How a state machine works\nA finite state machine (FSM) models something that can be in exactly one of several defined states at a time, and moves between \nthem only along allowed transitions.\n\nThree pieces:\n\nStates — the distinct \"modes\" your thing can be in. Only one is active at any moment. (e.g. Idle, Playing, Paused.)\nTransitions — the permitted arrows from one state to another. A transition fires on an event/trigger \n(a click, a timer, a signal from elsewhere in your patch).\n\nThe active state — the current mode. Everything downstream reacts to which state is active.\nSo the whole logic reduces to: \"I'm in state X; when event Y happens, go to state Z.\" Nothing else can happen. \nA traffic light is the classic example — Green → Yellow → Red → Green, and it physically cannot jump Green → Red.\n\nIn your Automata op, that's exactly the graph: nodes are states, arrows are transitions \n(with an optional frame delay), and the active state drives your visuals/particles.\n\nWhy use one instead of ad-hoc logic\nThe alternative most people reach for is a pile of booleans — isPlaying, isPaused, hasStarted, menuOpen… \nThe problem is those flags can combine into impossible or contradictory situations (isPlaying && isPaused?), \nand the logic to keep them consistent balloons into tangled if/else.\n\nA state machine fixes that:\n\nOnly one state at a time — illegal combinations are structurally impossible.\nExplicit, legal transitions — you can't reach a state except through a defined path, so no surprise jumps.\nVisible & self-documenting — the graph is the logic. Anyone can look at it and understand the behavior \n(this is a big deal for your op, since it's literally drawn on screen).\nPredictable & debuggable — at any moment you know precisely where you are and what can happen next; \nbugs become \"wrong arrow\" instead of \"which of 12 flags is stale?\"\nEasy to extend — adding a mode is adding a node and a few arrows, not threading a new flag through every conditional.\nWhere it shines: anything with distinct modes and controlled flow — interactive installations \n(attract-loop → interaction → reset), game/UI screens (menu → play → pause), sequenced animations, or timed show control. \nExactly the kind of thing cables patches do, which is why having it as a visual node graph is so handy: \nyou get the rigor of an FSM without writing the bookkeeping by hand."
                }
            ]
        },
